<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ibn Arabi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1165">1165</a>–<a href="/years/1240">1240</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Muhyiddin Ibn Arabi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ibn Arabi was an Andalusian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> mystic, philosopher and poet, born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murcia">Murcia</a> in 1165, who became one of the most influential thinkers in Islamic history. After years of travel in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghreb">Maghreb</a> he settled in the east and died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> in 1240. His works, including The Meccan Revelations and The Bezels of Wisdom, developed the doctrine later called the unity of being, and he became known as 'the greatest master'.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ibn-arabi.jpg" alt="Ibn Arabi" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Omar.Hafez · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D9%82%D8%A8%D8%B1_%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B4%D9%8A%D8%AE_%D9%85%D8%AD%D9%8A%D9%8A_%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%AF%D9%8A%D9%86_%D8%A8%D9%86_%D8%B9%D8%B1%D8%A8%D9%8A.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1919 the Spanish priest and scholar Miguel Asín Palacios argued that Dante drew on <b>Ibn Arabi</b> and on accounts of Muhammad's night journey, the Isra and Mi'raj, known in Latin as the Liber scalae Machometi; Francesco Gabrieli doubted it for lack of a means of transmission, while…</blockquote><a href="/work/divine-comedy">Divine Comedy · Reception</a></li><li><blockquote>Mértola passed to the Almohads, and his mystical writings were later studied by <b>Ibn Arabi</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/second-taifa-of-mertola">Second Taifa of Mértola</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murcia">Murcia</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/syria">Syria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.98,-1.13&amp;year=1213" aria-label="Murcia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4522 798 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ibn Arabi"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4972 1068h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4972 1068h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.98,-1.13&amp;year=1213">What was here in 1213? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;My heart is capable of every form, A cloister for the monk, a fane for idols, A pasture for gazelles, the pilgrim's Ka‘ba, The Tables of the Torah, the Koran.&#8221;</p><footer>Tarjuman al-Ashwaq (The Interpreter of Desires) &#183; tr. R. A. Nicholson, 1907 (A Literary History of the Arabs) &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/37985/pg37985-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Every one praises what he believes; his god is his own creature, and in praising it he praises himself.&#8221;</p><footer>On creeds (in Nicholson, The Mystics of Islam) &#183; tr. R. A. Nicholson, 1914 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/67388/pg67388-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;When my Beloved appears, With what eye do I see Him? With His eye, not with mine, For none sees Him except Himself.&#8221;</p><footer>A poem (in Nicholson, The Mystics of Islam) &#183; tr. R. A. Nicholson, 1914 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/67388/pg67388-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 539 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
